 You choose which of Legion's steps each issue of your tree runs, in a plan you write on the issue and give to Legion, unless a project's experiment drew the issue into a group whose issues run the project's default steps, which takes no plan from you (below). Legion's step roles, each with the type of its step, are planner (planning), implementer (implementation), tester (testing), reviewer (review) and acceptance (review), which this release takes only as `acceptance:owner`: that step is yours, your acceptance of the issue in `accepting`. Write the plan as the issue's `plan.md` document (`dispatch artifact` on the issue) with one `plan_task` block, whose `steps` attribute names the task's steps in the order they run, separated by spaces, and whose text says what the task must deliver: `:::plan_task{#a steps="implementer reviewer"}`. Once `plan.md` exists, upload each new version with `--force --precondition-json '{"document":"sha256:…"}'`, naming the token that `dispatch doc-read --issue <issue> --artifact plan.md` prints: an upload replaces every word of the document, so Dispatch refuses one that names no token (`400 PRECONDITION_REQUIRED`) and one whose token is behind the live version (`409 PRECONDITION_FAILED`), which keeps your version from erasing what a planner step wrote there since your read; on that refusal, read the plan again and upload again. This release runs one task for each issue, and its steps are some of `planner implementer tester reviewer acceptance:owner`, with `implementer` among them. Name the steps of a task in the order they run. A `planner` step may stand at any place, and the `implementer` step stands before each `tester`, `reviewer` and `acceptance:owner` step, since each of them reads the pull request the implementer opens. Legion runs the retro and the merge itself after the task's last step, so no plan names them. A task with no `steps`, or with `steps=""`, runs the project's default steps, `projects.<KEY>.default_steps` in its `legion.yaml`, which are `planner implementer tester reviewer acceptance:owner` unless the project sets others. Give each task block an id of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter or a digit, at most 64 bytes: Legion refuses any other id, since Dispatch records no step of such a task. Give the plan with the `legion` tool's `register_plan` (`issue`, `artifactId`, `version`), naming the document and the version you wrote. Legion reads that version and no later one, so a version you do not give changes nothing, and a planner step that writes its own tasks into `plan.md` changes nothing either. Legion refuses any other plan with `PLAN_REFUSED` and a reason for each cause, each naming its task where the cause is one task's: a reason that ends `This release of Legion does not carry it out yet.` names a plan a later release takes, so write the plan again within the rules above. Past the first reasons of one kind, the refusal counts the rest of that kind in one more reason: fix what it names and give the plan again, and the next refusal names what is left.
 
-For each step, decide whether a fresh agent works it or the agent of an earlier step, and write that choice and its reason in the task's text. This release gives each role one agent for each issue, the session that role keeps until the issue leaves the workflow, so it cannot carry that choice out yet; write it all the same, because it records what the work needed.
+Before you write a plan, read how the earlier plans of this project went: `dispatch plans --project <PROJECT>` prints the newest finished issues of the project that have a plan or a rating, each with its plan, each rating a retro gave that plan with its reason, its time and its count of failed steps; then say in the task's text what they showed that this plan follows or changes.
+
+For every step of every task, your own `acceptance:owner` step included, write in the task's text which agent works it, and why. That agent is a fresh agent, or the agent of an earlier step that you name, of this issue or of an earlier issue. The reason is what that agent knows, or must not know, for that step. A model or a model family says which model an agent runs on, and it is not the reason why a step needs a fresh agent or the agent of an earlier step. Choose the agent that the work needs, not the agent that this release can start. This release gives each role one agent for each issue, the session that role keeps until the issue leaves the workflow, so it cannot carry that choice out yet; write it all the same, because it records what the work needed. Where this release cannot start the agent you chose, or that agent's session has ended, write after that choice what Legion does in its place: this issue's one agent of the step's role works the step, and you work your own `acceptance:owner` step.
 
 Give each issue its plan before its work starts, but an issue whose experiment's group runs the default steps, which takes none: `read_record` shows a recorded issue's group, an assignment whose `plan` is `default`. For the root, call register_plan before register_gate: the gate's opening starts the root's first step. For a child, call register_plan before release_children releases it: Legion keeps the plan and starts the child's first step from it, unless the child, which a project's experiment draws when the daemon records it, falls into such a group, whose first step passes the plan over. An issue whose work started before you gave it a plan runs the task default, the project's default steps. To give it a plan, call stop_task on the task default, then register_plan with a version whose one task replaces it: Legion starts that task at its first step.
 
